 very small particles and  impaction in interception which are  efficient at capturing larger particles  are least efficient at that medium  particle size so 0.3 micrometer  particles are captured with 99.97  efficiency but smaller and larger  particles are also captured and even  with greater efficiency so this in  chamber hepa filtration system works as  a continuous dilution  so the entire  chamber air volume is is filtered  through that hepa filter every 60  seconds so that at within five minutes  after a 30 second door opening that  chamber inside the incubator reaches iso  class 5 clean room conditions  and as you can see the air continues to  get cleaner over time as the the door  remains closed  these hepa filters snap in and out  easily you don't need any special tools  and there's an alarm to tell you to  replace it after 12 months  all right so finally i want to finish up  talking about the incubator itself  because while i spent a lot of time  talking about the clean room and why  that's challenging and why we thought a  lot about that what it really comes down  to is if your cells don't grow properly  your therapy will not be successful so i  want to spend the last minutes talking  about  incubator performance and how that  affects cell growth  so  cell culture was developed with  immortalized cell lines such as the hela  cells from henrietta lacks and those  cells have been growing in culture for  so long i call them frankenstein cells  and they can survive many challenges  that we continually torture them with in  the laboratory but for cell therapy  we're using cells that are much much  more sensitive stem cells immune cells  and primary cells and these cells are  much more reactive to what's going on  around them than these immortalized cell  lines that have mutated and adjusted to  these kind of artificial conditions in  cell culture  so cells constantly check their  surroundings they're constantly  measuring temperature humidity  gas concentration